{
 "id": "2024-zhou-wayfinding",
 "name": "Wayfinding Signage Preferences: Designers vs Non-Designers (4 × 4 Choices)",
 "description": "Faithful replication of the questionnaire in Zhou, Ujang, Abd Manan & Abdul Aziz (2024), \"Bridging Perceptual Gaps: Designers vs. Non-Designers in Urban Wayfinding Signage Preferences\", Sustainability 16(22):9653. Part 1 sorts participants into designers and non-designers by their wayfinding-signage experience (course training only / design projects / completely unfamiliar) and records age group, gender and education. Part 2 shows the paper's four sets of four simulated street signs (made by the authors in Photoshop 2022 and Blender 3.0): amount of text information, sign shape, colour coding, and decorative patterns. For each set participants pick the sign they prefer for urban environments and explain why in an open answer. In the paper (N = 321 in Nanning, China; chi-square tests by group), most people chose text + English + destination icon + arrow (57.3%), the multi-directional shape (51.1%), multicolour coding (58.6%) and a small number of local-meaning patterns (68.8%), with designers and non-designers differing on several sets. Images are cropped from the paper's Figures 1–4 (CC BY 4.0); credit to the authors, and the template will be removed if the authors object. Differences from the original study: (1) the questions are in English (the original survey ran in China; the signs keep their Chinese text); (2) each image is shown with its option label from the paper's figure captions; (3) a consent page is added and every question is required.",
